This PR contains the next changes:

#### Renamed OpenAPIConfig -> ClientConfig

#### ClientConfig config has lowercase properties now

Instead of
```
export type ClientConfig = {
    BASE?: string;
    VERSION?: string;
    WITH_CREDENTIALS?: boolean;
    TOKEN?: string | Resolver<string>;
    USERNAME?: string | Resolver<string>;
    PASSWORD?: string | Resolver<string>;
    HEADERS?: Headers | Resolver<Headers>;
}
```
it will be 
```
export type ClientConfig = {
    baseUrl?: string;
    version?: string;
    withCredentials?: boolean;
    token?: string | Resolver<string>;
    username?: string | Resolver<string>;
    password?: string | Resolver<string>;
    headers?: Headers | Resolver<Headers>;
}
```

#### The httpClient is now passed as an instance object and not a constructor class

Instead of this:
```
export class AccountServiceClient {
  ...
  readonly request: BaseHttpRequest;

  constructor(
    openApiConfig: OpenAPIConfig,
    HttpRequest: new (config: OpenAPIConfig) => BaseHttpRequest,
  ) {
    this.request = new HttpRequest({
      BASE: openApiConfig.BASE ?? 'http://localhost:4000',
      VERSION: openApiConfig.VERSION ?? 'x.x.x',
      WITH_CREDENTIALS: openApiConfig.WITH_CREDENTIALS ?? false,
      TOKEN: openApiConfig.TOKEN,
      USERNAME: openApiConfig.USERNAME,
      PASSWORD: openApiConfig.PASSWORD,
      HEADERS: openApiConfig.HEADERS,
    });
    ...
}
```
It will now generate this:
```
export class AccountServiceClient {
    ...
    constructor(openApiConfig: OpenAPIConfig, httpClient: BaseHttpRequest) {
        const config = {
            baseUrl: openApiConfig?.baseUrl ?? 'http://localhost:4000',
            version: openApiConfig?.version ?? 'x.x.x',
            withCredentials: openApiConfig?.withCredentials ?? false,
            token: openApiConfig?.token,
            username: openApiConfig?.username,
            password: openApiConfig?.password,
            headers: openApiConfig?.headers,
        }
        this.organizations = new OrganizationsService(httpClient, config);
        ...
    }
}
```
So now we pass an instance of the httpClient implementation instead of creating it in the client itself. This allows us to reuse the same HTTP client instance between all potential clients. Should not matter much for usage but allows us to be flexible in the future.

#### The httpClient is not not dependent on the OpenApi configuration

The BaseHttpRequest is now an interface with one `request` method that receives the OpenApi configuration as a parameter. It also receives additional optional config in case we need to override some config values. 
```
export interface BaseHttpRequest {
    request(
        options: ApiRequestOptions,
        config: OpenAPIConfig,
        mergeConfig?: OpenAPIConfig
    ): Promise<ApiResult>;
}
```

All services now has one base OpenAPIConfig with the default information for service (e.g. baseUrl) and also there is a possibility to override some values for every request, every method now has the last optional parameter to specify the OpenAPIConfig. For example, this allows us to send additional information with different headers only for one particular request. Since it is optional, there is no harm in having that but it provides flexibility.

```
export class OrganizationsService {
    private readonly httpRequest: BaseHttpRequest;
    private readonly openApiConfig: OpenAPIConfig;

    constructor(httpRequest: BaseHttpRequest, openApiConfig: OpenAPIConfig) {
        this.httpRequest = httpRequest;
        this.openApiConfig = openApiConfig;
    }

    public async createOrganization(
        requestBody: CreateOrganizationRequestBody,
        config?: OpenAPIConfig
    ): Promise<ApiResult<OrganizationResponse>> {
        return this.httpRequest.request({
            method: 'POST',
            path: `/organizations`,
            body: requestBody,
            mediaType: 'application/json',
        }, this.openApiConfig, config);
    }
}
```

#### The request calls now return full information about the HTTP results, not only the body.

So response changed from `Promise<MyCustomResponse>` to `Promise<ApiResult<MyCustomResponse>>`.
E.g. the usage will change from 
```
const res: OrganizationResponse = await client.organizations.createOrganization({
  name: 'MyTestOrg',
  description: 'My Test Org Description',
})
```
to 
```
const res: OrganizationResponse = (
      await client.organizations.createOrganization({
        name: 'MyTestOrg',
        description: 'My Test Org Description',
      })
).body;
```
The body has proper typings, so you will see all the response fields there.

This change is needed to provide more details about the server response, not only the body but also headers and status. 
This does not significantly change the usage of the client but again provides more flexibility.

Fixes #86.

Since the lifetime parameter of `Cow` is only for the borrowed variant, it is irrelevant for the `impl`. This also allows for `Cow<'static, str>` as can be seen when testing with the following code:
```rust


#[derive(Arbitrary)]
pub struct StaticCow {
    cow: Cow<'static, str>,
}

#[test]
fn static_cow() {
    // Last byte is used for length
    let raw: Vec<u8> = vec![97, 98, 99, 100, 3];
    let static_cow: StaticCow = arbitrary_from(&raw);
    assert_eq!(
        Cow::<'static, str>::Owned("abc".to_string()),
        static_cow.cow
    );

    let (lower, upper) = <StaticCow as Arbitrary>::size_hint(0);
    assert_eq!(lower, 8);
    assert_eq!(upper, None);
}
```
If desirable I can also add this test case to `tests/derive.rs` (which is also where `arbitrary_from` is taken from).
